P('jane', 'janeeyre', '“I tell you I must go!” I retorted', 'equal,—as we are!”', 'Jane Eyre', 'Charlotte Brontë', 1847, [
  ('Why does Jane say she must go?', 'She cannot stay if she is to become nothing to Rochester', ['She has a new job', 'She is ill', 'She is going on holiday'], '“Do you think I can stay to become nothing to you?”'),
  ('Jane asks, “Do you think I am an automaton?” What is an automaton?', 'A machine without feelings', ['A servant', 'A ghost', 'A child'], 'She explains it herself: “a machine without feelings.”'),
  ('Which qualities does Jane say do not make her “soulless and heartless”?', 'Being poor, obscure, plain, and little', ['Being rich and beautiful', 'Being proud and rude', 'Being young and foolish'], '“Do you think, because I am poor, obscure, plain, and little, I am soulless and heartless?”'),
  ('What is Jane’s main claim in this speech?', 'She is Rochester’s equal in spirit', ['She is better than Rochester', 'She wants his money', 'She is afraid of him'], 'She says “it is my spirit that addresses your spirit,” standing “equal,—as we are!”'),
  ('What device do “Do you think …?” questions repeated several times create?', 'Rhetorical questions that build passion', ['A simile', 'Understatement', 'Comic relief'], 'She does not want answers; the repeated questions make her point forcefully.'),
])
P('heathcliff', 'wuthering', 'My love for Linton is like the foliage in the woods', 'but as my own being.', 'Wuthering Heights', 'Emily Brontë', 1847, [
  ('How does Catherine describe her love for Linton?', 'Like leaves that change with time', ['Like rocks that never change', 'Like a fire that burns forever', 'Like a river that runs dry'], '“My love for Linton is like the foliage in the woods: time will change it.”'),
  ('How does she describe her love for Heathcliff?', 'Like the eternal rocks beneath — not delightful, but necessary', ['Like flowers in spring', 'Like a passing storm', 'Like summer leaves'], 'It “resembles the eternal rocks beneath: a source of little visible delight, but necessary.”'),
  ('What does Catherine mean by “I am Heathcliff”?', 'She feels he is part of her very self', ['She is pretending to be him', 'She has changed her name', 'She hates him'], 'He is in her mind “not as a pleasure … but as my own being.”'),
  ('What two kinds of love does the contrast of foliage and rocks set up?', 'Changeable surface love versus deep, lasting love', ['Love of nature versus love of money', 'Young love versus old love', 'True love versus false friendship'], 'Leaves change with the seasons; rocks endure.'),
])

# ---------------- Poems — Grades 5–12 ----------------
P('hope', 'dickinson', 'Hope is the thing with feathers', 'It asked a crumb of me.', '“Hope” is the thing with feathers', 'Emily Dickinson', 1891, [
  ('What is hope compared to in this poem?', 'A bird', ['A flower', 'A star', 'A river'], 'Hope is “the thing with feathers” that “perches in the soul.”'),
  ('This kind of comparison, without “like” or “as,” is called …', 'an extended metaphor', ['a simile', 'onomatopoeia', 'alliteration'], 'Hope is treated as a bird all through the poem, without using “like” or “as.”'),
  ('When does the bird’s song sound “sweetest”?', 'In the gale — during the hardest times', ['In the morning', 'In summer', 'When the poet is happy'], '“And sweetest in the gale is heard.”'),
  ('What does the last stanza say hope asks for in return?', 'Nothing — not even a crumb', ['A song', 'A little food', 'A warm place to sleep'], '“Yet, never, in extremity, / It asked a crumb of me.”'),
  ('What is the poem’s message about hope?', 'Hope keeps us going in hard times and asks nothing back', ['Hope is easily lost', 'Hope is only for the young', 'Hope is like a cage'], 'It sings “and never stops at all,” even in storms and cold seas.'),
], poem=True)
P('flanders', 'mccrae', 'In Flanders fields the poppies blow', 'We shall not sleep, though poppies grow In Flanders fields.', 'In Flanders Fields', 'John McCrae (Canadian)', 1915, [
  ('Who is speaking in this poem?', 'Soldiers who have died in the war', ['A farmer in Flanders', 'A general giving orders', 'A child picking poppies'], '“We are the Dead. Short days ago / We lived.”'),
  ('Why is the poppy a symbol of Remembrance Day in Canada?', 'This poem pictures poppies growing among soldiers’ graves', ['Poppies bloom in November', 'Poppies were the soldiers’ food', 'The army used poppies as a badge'], '“The poppies blow / Between the crosses, row on row.”'),
  ('What contrast does the first stanza make?', 'Larks still sing above, while the guns roar below', ['Day and night', 'Summer and winter', 'Victory and defeat'], 'The larks “still bravely singing, fly / Scarce heard amid the guns below.”'),
  ('What do the dead ask the living to do?', 'Carry on the fight — “the torch”', ['Forget the war', 'Plant more poppies', 'Go home in peace'], '“To you from failing hands we throw / The torch; be yours to hold it high.”'),
  ('What does “we shall not sleep” mean in the last stanza?', 'The dead will not rest in peace if the living break faith with them', ['The soldiers are on night watch', 'The guns are too loud', 'The poppies keep them awake'], '“If ye break faith with us who die / We shall not sleep.”'),
], poem=True)
P('mcgee', 'service', 'There are strange things done in the midnight sun', 'the only one to whimper was Sam McGee.', 'The Cremation of Sam McGee', 'Robert W. Service', 1907, [
  ('Where is this poem set?', 'In the Yukon / Arctic north during the gold rush', ['In Tennessee', 'In a big city', 'On a tropical island'], 'It tells of “the men who moil for gold” on “the Dawson trail.”'),
  ('What is the speaker hinting at in the first stanza?', 'An eerie tale about cremating Sam McGee', ['A funny story about a dog race', 'How to find gold', 'A love story'], '“The queerest they ever did see / Was that night on the marge of Lake Lebarge / I cremated Sam McGee.”'),
  ('Where was Sam McGee from?', 'Tennessee', ['The Yukon', 'California', 'Lake Lebarge'], '“Now Sam McGee was from Tennessee.”'),
  ('What does the rhyme inside lines like “the Northern Lights have seen queer sights” create?', 'A strong, galloping rhythm (internal rhyme)', ['A sad, slow mood', 'A free-verse feel', 'No pattern at all'], '“Lights” and “sights” rhyme within the same line.'),
  ('Why is it ironic that Sam always said he would “sooner live in hell”?', 'He hates the cold so much he would rather be somewhere hot', ['He loves the cold', 'He was a preacher', 'He lived in Tennessee'], 'He “was always cold” in the frozen north.'),
], poem=True, nth=2)
P('road', 'frost', 'Two roads diverged in a yellow wood,', 'And that has made all the difference.', 'The Road Not Taken', 'Robert Frost', 1916, [
  ('What choice does the speaker face?', 'Which of two paths in a wood to take', ['Whether to go home', 'Which friend to visit', 'Whether to cut down a tree'], '“Two roads diverged in a yellow wood.”'),
  ('What do the two roads stand for?', 'Choices in life', ['Two real highways', 'Two seasons', 'Two friends'], 'The roads are a metaphor for life decisions.'),
  ('Were the two roads really very different?', 'No — they were worn “really about the same”', ['Yes — one was much busier', 'Yes — one was paved', 'The poem doesn’t say'], '“Though as for that the passing there / Had worn them really about the same.”'),
  ('Why does the speaker say he will tell this “with a sigh”?', 'He knows he can never know what the other road held', ['He is tired from walking', 'He is sad the wood was yellow', 'He got lost'], 'He doubted “if I should ever come back.”'),
  ('Why is the title “The Road Not Taken” and not “The Road Taken”?', 'The poem is really about the choice he will always wonder about', ['He never walked at all', 'The road was closed', 'It is a mistake'], 'The untaken path is what haunts him.'),
], poem=True)

# ---------------- Shakespeare — Grades 8–12 ----------------
